WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:05.370
Blade Runner 2049 seemed like a surefire hit,
with two Hollywood favorites sharing the lead,

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:08.760
tons of critical praise, and a respectable
box office forecast.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:13.920
However, the long-awaited sequel brought in
just $31.5 million on opening weekend — which

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.039
is a pretty dismal return compared to its
$150 million production budget.

00:00:18.039 --> 00:00:20.319
So, why did this film fizzle out in the theaters?

00:00:20.319 --> 00:00:24.759
Let's took a look at what went wrong with
Blade Runner 2049.

00:00:24.759 --> 00:00:25.759
Run time

00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:31.519
Blade Runner 2049 comes in at a whopping 163
minutes, which seems to have been just long

00:00:31.519 --> 00:00:34.560
enough to dissuade casual viewers from a trip
to the theater.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.230
While sci-fi fans might still be willing to
spend that much time with the movie, those

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:41.770
just looking for a fun time would probably
be more in the mood for something a bit on

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:42.960
the shorter side.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.640
Not only that, but the number of showings
each theater can host per day is significantly

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.789
lessened by its length, which can lead to
a loss of revenue.

00:00:49.789 --> 00:00:54.089
Overall, when it comes to movies, longer isn't
always better.

00:00:54.089 --> 00:00:55.129
Sequel slump

00:00:55.129 --> 00:00:59.480
Hollywood's all about reboots and sequels,
but usually the underlying property is something

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:01.289
that was successful the first time around.

00:01:01.289 --> 00:01:05.850
While 1982's Blade Runner has since amassed
an intense cult following, it's easy to forget

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:09.210
that it didn't actually perform very well
during its brief theatrical run.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:12.990
Overshadowed by E.T.: The Extra Terrestrial,
the film stayed in theaters for just five

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.090
weeks and earned only $32.9 million…

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:19.030
just about $82 million if you adjust for inflation.

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:23.049
And not only was the first film only mildly
popular upon release, but there's since been

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:28.700
several different cuts released on video and
DVD, like the "Final Cut" released in 2007.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:33.750
All those cuts are confusing to casual fans,
and meant those potential moviegoers for 2049

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:37.799
had to decide between watching one of at least
three different versions of the original before

00:01:37.799 --> 00:01:39.149
heading out to the theater.

00:01:39.149 --> 00:01:42.390
That's a whole lotta homework, especially
for a weekend.

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:43.890
Bloated budget

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:48.109
Given the lackluster initial interest in the
original, it was a risky move for Warner Bros.

00:01:48.109 --> 00:01:51.290
to invest so much capital in the sequel, 25
years later.

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:56.030
1982's Blade Runner was made on a big budget
for its time, costing a reported $30 million

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:59.929
to make, which translates to about $76 million
today.

00:01:59.929 --> 00:02:04.560
But Blade Runner 2049 is estimated to have
cost twice that amount, after tax rebates.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.270
That might've allowed for gorgeous visuals
and all, but it was also a risky investment

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:12.720
for a movie that was 25 years overdue and
leaning on a property that wasn't a financial

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.670
runaway to begin with.

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.330
Vague marketing

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:20.370
One common thread among recent box office
flops is marketing that focuses on visuals

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:21.910
over substance.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:25.230
Thanks to big bombs like Valerian and the
City of a Thousand Planets and Ghost in the

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:29.860
Shell, audiences have grown wary of bad films
being disguised with sweeping camera shots

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:30.860
and a big budget.

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:34.800
While this didn't end up being the case with
Blade Runner 2049, the trailers might not

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.560
have drummed as much buzz as they could have
thanks to how they highlight the movie's looks

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:39.690
over its plot.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:44.070
Of course, the entire plot of the movie itself
was a spoiler, but keeping details under wraps

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:48.440
did them no favors in terms of bringing in
viewers unfamiliar with the source material.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:50.870
"Too many questions."

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:52.340
Too faithful

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:55.510
There's a lot of complicated mythology at
play in the Blade Runner films.

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:59.260
Many fans and critics were delightfully surprised
to see how well the sequel dealt with it,

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:03.040
with the film answering and expanding on some
of the deeper questions asked in the original.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:07.210
However, that type of introspection and that
deep connection to the mythology makes it

00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:08.920
daunting for the unconverted.

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:12.800
The mythos of Blade Runner is so deep and
complicated that it can keep casual viewers

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.490
at arm's length — and 2049 may have stuck
too close to the source material to have the

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:20.480
kind of mass appeal it was aiming for.

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:21.650
Intense competition

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:26.000
Blade Runner 2049 had trouble attracting young
audiences, and that may be in part thanks

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.240
to its R rating.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.960
After years of shying away from adult-oriented
fare, studios have been more willing to embrace

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.670
it lately — in fact, it seems like those
films have started flooding the marketplace.

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:40.120
But 2049 had to stand up against R-rated competition
like the still-popular It, American Assassin,

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:44.550
and Kingsman: The Golden Circle, all of which
were much easier to digest than Blade Runner.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:47.670
Going for a less strict rating would have
helped to cut out some of the competition

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:51.520
— and likely wouldn't have seriously cut
into the film's quality considering the movie

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:54.580
is fairly tame for its R-rating.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:55.880
What now?

00:03:55.880 --> 00:04:00.450
Blade Runner 2049, like many a domestic flop,
now turns to the international box office

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:01.480
for salvation.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:07.280
The movie brought in $50.2 million overseas
during its debut, just about on par with expectations,

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.820
although not enough to make up for its domestic
failure.

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:13.980
The movie's biggest hope lies in Asian market
releases, which come later in October and

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:14.980
November.

00:04:14.980 --> 00:04:18.919
Nations like South Korea and China are more
friendly to movies with big-budget visuals,

00:04:18.919 --> 00:04:22.840
so if the film is able to bring in big bucks
overseas, it could still be profitable.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:26.500
And while Hollywood is a business, even if
the film's receipts aren't as strong as the

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:30.560
studio might've hoped, the movie definitely
has cult favorite potential, just like its

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:31.560
predecessor.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:32.560
So who knows?

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:37.590
We might just be seeing a third Blade Runner
installment drop...in another 25 years.

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:38.590
Thanks for watching!

